.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Clapeyron&#x27;s 1834 combined gas law unites Boyle&#x27;s law (PV constant at fixed T), Charles&#x27;s and Gay-Lussac&#x27;s law (V \propto T at fixed P), and Avogadro&#x27;s hypothesis into PV_m = RT. This example draws both classic views with IdealGas: hyperbolic Boyle isotherms, and straight Charles isobars that all extrapolate to zero volume at absolute zero.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/thermo/equations_of_state/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_ideal_gas_law_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/thermo/equations_of_state/plot_01_ideal_gas_law`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Clapeyron's ideal gas law, PV = RT</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/thermo/equations_of_state/plot_01_ideal_gas_law`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Redlich and Kwong divided van der Waals&#x27;s attraction term by \sqrt{T}. This example draws CO2 P-V isotherms well above and just below the critical temperature from RedlichKwong, with VanDerWaals and IdealGas for comparison. Both real-gas equations are built from CO2&#x27;s critical temperature and pressure alone via from_critical_constants. Redlich-Kwong&#x27;s universal critical compressibility factor is 1/3, closer to real gases than van der Waals&#x27;s 3/8.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/thermo/equations_of_state/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_03_redlich_kwong_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/thermo/equations_of_state/plot_03_redlich_kwong`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Redlich-Kwong isotherms of CO2 against van der Waals and the ideal gas</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/thermo/equations_of_state/plot_03_redlich_kwong`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="van&#x27;t Hoff found that a dilute solution&#x27;s osmotic pressure (~chemistrykit.thermo.systems.mixtures.osmotic_pressure) equals the pressure the solute particles would exert as an ideal gas at the same concentration and temperature. The factor i counts particles per formula unit: 1 for sucrose, about 2 for NaCl. The same factor multiplies the other colligative effects, freezing-point depression and boiling-point elevation (~chemistrykit.thermo.systems.mixtures.freezing_point_depression, boiling_point_elevation).">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/thermo/mixtures/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_03_osmotic_pressure_thumb.png
